statement:
A theory consists of a finite set of laws and a finite set of theorems. Each law and each theorem is a finite string of symbols. A theorem is said to be simple if its proof consists only of laws, and otherwise it is said to be complex. A complex theorem can be used in the proof of another theorem.

Suppose that:

(i) every theorem can be proved;

(ii) every theorem has exactly one proof;

(iii) every proof uses at most one complex theorem.

Show that the number of theorems is at most the number of laws.
